{
  "image_name": "0e729b647a16202b8b87e533b6220bed.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes several ways RANKL antagonists may enhance anti-tumor immunity within the tumor microenvironment and beyond. At the center is a tumor mass embedded among non-malignant tissue, with surrounding immune cells, and the dashed callouts explain different proposed mechanisms.\n\nOne theme is cross-modulation of T cells in the tumor environment: activated CD4+ and CD8+ T cells appear to express more RANKL, especially alongside anti-PD-1 treatment, and blocking RANKL may alter these immune interactions. Another major mechanism is inhibition of suppressive RANK+ myeloid cells. RANKL from immune, tumor, or stromal cells activates these myeloid cells, which then suppress CD4+, CD8+, and NK cells. The figure indicates that RANKL blockade can relieve this suppression, increase type 1 inflammatory cytokines such as IFNγ, TNF, and IL-2, and promote T-cell infiltration.\n\nA separate panel highlights effects in the thymus, where RANKL antagonists may disrupt central tolerance by affecting negative selection and regulatory T-cell generation, thereby increasing more active CD4+ and CD8+ cells with higher granzyme B and Ki67. Finally, the figure suggests a possible direct effect on RANKL-expressing T cells themselves through altered or reverse signaling.",
